  "tour": {
    "title": "Wrath at the Cross: Three Christian Readings",
    "intro": "You asked to hear teachers disagree, so this tour starts with a clear affirmative answer, then widens the frame: one teacher argues that penal substitution is real but belongs inside Christus Victor, while another says the Bible’s story is not that God punished Jesus at all. Listen for where they agree—Jesus bears sin and delivers people—and where they place the meaning of wrath.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:bacb1fc2-com-e79bf8f7-6ab5-30f3-9e4f-46bbb41dd164",
        "startSec": 1320,
        "endSec": 1530,
        "why": "Start with the most direct yes. The teacher reads Romans 3:25 through the Day of Atonement and says that “the wrath of God that sin deserves” was “poured out on Jesus Himself,” so the penalty is dealt with in Christ. This gives you a defined penal-substitution account before hearing qualifications and objections.",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans 3:21-26",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/zhvgac/romans_321-368t8hu.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1954,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:32b053a0-4d5c-11ee-8683-f1dd20c5e5ec",
        "startSec": 1320,
        "endSec": 1620,
        "why": "N. T. Wright resists treating the options as either/or. He says the cross is victory over “the forces of darkness,” achieved by Jesus “taking the place of the sinner,” and explicitly calls this “definitely substitutionary” and “definitely penal”—but only within the larger story of God defeating evil. This complicates the first clip rather than simply reversing it.",
        "episodeTitle": "#185 Questions on Crucifixion and Atonement (Classic)",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR9770585049.mp3?updated=1749653873",
        "durationSec": 2261,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "radically-christian:podcast:e9106bdd-https-radicallychristian-com-p-9457",
        "startSec": 1600,
        "endSec": 1900,
        "why": "This conversation lays the competing models beside each other. It first says “love is the primary engine behind the cross,” then describes satisfaction as settling a debt and penal substitution as a courtroom punishment Jesus takes in our place. The speakers also acknowledge that their description can sound like a caricature, which helps you hear both the attraction and the danger they see in the wrath-centered version.",
        "episodeTitle": "Theories of Atonement – CrossTalk S4E10",
        "sourceId": "radically-christian",
        "source": "Radically Christian",
        "audioUrl": "https://substackcdn.com/video_upload/post/148682238/4202feae-310f-48d3-8260-2bd297e7ec54/transcoded.mp3?post_id=148682238&relation=podcast&Expires=1785437879&Key-Pair-Id=APKAIVDA3NPSMPSPESQQ&Signature=E81CSoqx5J2xZCnTPRkPrWvRg17tmjM-2SKhycuZSAMk4XOl732-KwOVFWlcApEglSliBQA4R8rvfsVLBkCl5wLrSJeFJE8YFypZehk1S-ztVhFWUOaXbBbN9Ljnj9yk4CxD8CvqZvUeUpm0st083KKQu8uajFMmkOZhz7FeZXgC6Xmc9frzupTiWp9gV1U1qj14AnTiRqjJoBIVkwTkQzHh0KCRmLXCs0pKbEAEz-H7qAjGYKr0-w04CQCM2GTYNZ2q8WWm8SHYOfihsTvcGwCbh48uSLygBXDGvyuPHDZzeDCDWp8K5zjgnJbXnWNCyt8aDP-dX-e4oZzV77TM1Q__",
        "durationSec": 2917,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:day-lord-part-5-jesus-and-war-against-evil",
        "startSec": 1836,
        "endSec": 2068,
        "why": "Now hear the sharpest disagreement. BibleProject says plainly, “Paul doesn’t have the idea that God punished Jesus”; instead, “God punished sin in the human existence of Jesus,” as Jesus suffers the consequences of human evil and is vindicated in resurrection. The clip reframes the cross around mortality, violence, death, and the defeat of evil rather than a Father redirecting punishment onto an innocent Son.",
        "episodeTitle": "Jesus and the War Against Evil",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/31a59f79-101f-4f18-92e5-f9cd6b8aa32b/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=31a59f79-101f-4f18-92e5-f9cd6b8aa32b",
        "durationSec": 3392,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:saved-from-gods-wrath",
        "startSec": 2338,
        "endSec": 2580,
        "why": "Close by returning to the phrase “saved from God’s wrath.” This teacher argues that Paul foregrounds “God’s love,” and interprets wrath from Romans 1 as being handed over to destructive desires and a deathward way of life—not simply God deciding to kill someone instead of you. The ending connects salvation through Jesus’ death with salvation through “his life,” leaving you with the practical and theological stakes of the disagreement.",
        "episodeTitle": "Saved from God’s Wrath",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/2c920bb5-7256-4ada-b28c-d39f072152d5/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=2c920bb5-7256-4ada-b28c-d39f072152d5",
        "durationSec": 3397,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You have now heard three distinct emphases: wrath and penalty borne by Jesus, penalty held together with victory over the powers, and a rejection of the idea that God punished Jesus in favor of God confronting sin, death, and violence in Christ. The disagreement is not over whether the cross saves, but over what “wrath,” “punishment,” and “bearing sin” mean—and what picture of God each account gives you.",
